        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Dye-based stains utilize completely dissolved colorant molecules that are small enough to penetrate deep into the microscopic pores of wood cell walls, resulting in a highly transparent finish that highlights the natural grain. Pigment-based wood stains rely on insoluble particulate colorants suspended in a binder. These pigments are physically too large to penetrate the dense cellular structure of the wood and instead deposit in the surface pores and grain crevices, partially obscuring the grain under a protective film."
