      <!-- FAQ SECTION -->
      <section class="section" id="faq" style="border-top: 2px solid var(--border); padding-top: 40px; margin-top: 40px;">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px; margin-top: 20px;">
          
          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">1. What is the primary difference in wood penetration between dye-based and pigment-based wood stains?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Dye-based stains utilize completely dissolved colorant molecules that are small enough to penetrate deep into the microscopic pores of wood cell walls, resulting in a highly transparent finish that highlights the natural grain. Pigment-based wood stains rely on insoluble particulate colorants suspended in a binder. These pigments are physically too large to penetrate the dense cellular structure of the wood and instead deposit in the surface pores and grain crevices, partially obscuring the grain under a protective film.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">2. How does a 2K polyurethane wood coating differ from a 1K polyurethane coating?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              A two-component (2K) polyurethane coating cures via a chemical crosslinking reaction between Part A (hydroxyl-functional acrylic or polyester polyol) and Part B (aliphatic polyisocyanate hardener), forming a highly chemical- and wear-resistant thermoset film. A one-component (1K) polyurethane coating cures either physically by solvent evaporation (in thermoplastic PUDs) or chemically by reaction with atmospheric moisture or oxygen (in oil-modified urethanes), resulting in a lower crosslink density and lower overall chemical resistance.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">3. What is the polymerization mechanism of UV-cure wood finishes?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              UV-cure wood finishes cure through free-radical polymerization. Exposure to intense ultraviolet light causes photoinitiators to cleave into highly reactive free radicals. These radicals immediately attack the carbon-carbon double bonds in acrylate oligomers (such as urethane or epoxy acrylates) and reactive monomer diluents, triggering a chain-reaction crosslinking process that solidifies the liquid coating into a dense polymer network within seconds.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">4. Why is nitrocellulose lacquer still used despite its low chemical resistance compared to polyurethanes?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Nitrocellulose (NC) lacquers remain popular in musical instrument manufacturing and fine antique furniture restoration due to their excellent clarity, rapid drying times, and ease of repair. NC lacquers cure purely by physical solvent evaporation, meaning that newly applied coats solvent-blend or melt into the underlying layers. This allows for seamless repairs and polishing to a high-gloss finish, despite being sensitive to moisture, alcohol, and mechanical wear.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">5. How do self-crosslinking water-based acrylic-polyurethane dispersions work?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Self-crosslinking dispersions incorporate functional carbonyl groups (such as diacetone acrylamide, DAAM) on the polymer backbone alongside a co-reactive hydrazide (like adipic acid dihydrazide, ADH) in the water phase. While in dispersion, the water prevents reaction. As the film dries and water evaporates, the local concentration of DAAM and ADH rises, triggering a room-temperature keto-hydrazide crosslinking reaction that significantly elevates the film density and chemical resistance.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">6. What are reactive diluents in UV-cure wood finishes, and why are they necessary?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Reactive diluents are low-viscosity, multi-functional acrylate monomers (such as HDDA, TPGDA, or TMPTA) added to UV formulations. They serve a dual purpose: they physically reduce the viscosity of high-molecular-weight oligomers to allow for spray or roller application, and they chemically react during UV exposure to become an integral part of the crosslinked polymer network, eliminating the need for volatile organic solvents.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>
      </section>

